    "etymology": {
     "text": "From Middle High German niwiht, niweht, niht, a contracted form of Old High German niowiht, from Proto-West Germanic *naiwwiht; essentially nio (“never”) + wiht (“being, creature, thing”) – literally “not a thing”.",
     "src": "Wiktionary (kaikki.org)"
    },
    "funFacts": [
     "English not has the same history: from Old English nāwiht “no thing” (also the source of naught).",
     "Greek δεν, Russian не and Turkish -me/-ma stand before the verb or inside it; German nicht usually comes late in the clause: Ich trinke den Kaffee nicht.",
     "nicht never combines with another negative word in standard German: Ich esse nichts (not *Ich esse nicht nichts) – unlike Greek δεν τρώω τίποτα or Russian я ничего не ем."
    ],
    "pitfalls": [
     "nicht vs kein: kein replaces “not a / not any” before a noun without the definite article (Ich habe keine Gurke), nicht negates verbs, adjectives and nouns with der/die/das (Ich esse die Gurke nicht).",
     "Position: nicht usually comes after the object and at the end of a main clause (Ich mag ihn nicht), but before adjectives, places and second verbs (nicht frisch, nicht hier, nicht essen)."
    ],
    "subtleties": [
     "The position of nicht can change what is negated: Ich esse nicht die Gurke (but something else) vs Ich esse die Gurke nicht (not at all).",
     "nicht before a word negates just that word: nicht hier (but there), nicht gut, nicht mein Bruder."
    ]
   },
